WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.160
Tim: Our next lecture topic is: Churchill
as a defender of constitutionalism. To address

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.720
that topic please welcome the 12th president
of Hillsdale College, Dr. Larry P. Arnn.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:30.000
Larry: Thank you. Good morning. Can everybody
hear okay? I’ve come to learn in recent

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:39.250
years that nobody can hear me when I talk
and that's one reason why I'm popular. To

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.460
people in the front I've heard them talk to
the people in the back, and the people in

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:52.250
the front say, “No, he wasn’t that good.”
Thank you Tim. This popped into my mind while

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:58.380
Tim was talking. Tim played football at Hillsdale
College a little bit. He didn’t keep playing.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.500
I asked him why because as you can see he’s
a really big guy and he said well he was more

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.460
academically inclined. John Cervini who was
an All-American football player at Hillsdale

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:16.290
College gives a different account of why he
didn’t play so much. They were both really

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:24.080
great students.
I said last night that the study of Churchill

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.780
and the few people who are like him is a good
thing for everybody to do. I said that it's

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.909
a good thing for our college to do because
of its interest in civil and religious freedom.

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:39.960
I said that everybody needs to know what statesmanship
is. I'm going to try to put these arguments

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:46.140
together and tell you what I think Churchill
was about in his life, and I'll mention his

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:53.440
interest in constitutionalism along the way.
At the beginning I want to make two distinctions.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:02:01.000
One is between constitutionalism and statesmanship.
Actually on the other order, the one is what

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:08.060
do statesmen do, what is their gift, what
faculty do they exercise? We learn in the

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:16.240
classic books that the gift that we have for
choosing is called prudence. Prudence is a

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:25.150
contradictory thing. It involves two things
at the same time. One is what we aim for,

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:33.319
what we love, what we seek. The other is all
the circumstances around us. Those two things

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:41.170
get in the way of each other all the time.
If you have children, they play the clarinet,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:50.480
or they sing in the choir, or they play soccer
or basketball and you want to go. But also

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.560
you got to work. You have clients. There’s
a man in this room today who's a chief engineer

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:03.840
at NASA and the rockets might blow up if he
didn’t go to work. So he's got a pick. Both

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.970
of those things are absolute commitments.
You make contract with somebody. You’re

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:13.880
supposed to do what you say. Give birth to
a child. You’re supposed to take care of

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:19.319
the child. But the two things compete with
each other in time and you got to try to solve

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:25.450
that problem.
The way I solved it was marrying above myself.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.860
My wife is so nice that she can be nice to
everybody, all at the same time, including

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:34.200
the children especially. But that's the kind
of thing.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:46.099
Here’s a choice that Churchill made. It
gets to be 1940. He becomes Prime Minister

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:53.680
on the day Hitler comes to the West. The war
had been slow in the West up until that day.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.110
But that day the government had broken the
day before. Churchill who was a member of

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:03.550
the government that broke had defended the
Prime Minister and then he becomes Prime Minister

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:10.849
and he reads that the German army is coming.
May the 10th, 1940 is the day he became Prime

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:17.359
Minister and the German army comes in a hurry.
France, one of the greatest nations in the

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:24.309
world with one of the largest armies in the
world falls in about six weeks. Churchill

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:32.820
watches all that happen. Over the course of
about 18 days the British come to understand

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.520
that they're going to have to fight the Germans
by themselves.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:43.169
Germany by the way is in league at this time
with Soviet Russia, so it's both of them.

00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:48.449
The British have nothing. I mean they have
10 divisions, something like that. The Germans

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:56.379
have about 300. They're going to be alone.
The ones who got back from Dunkirk this unfolds,

00:04:56.379 --> 00:05:03.259
that's over by June the fourth, 1940, they
didn't get back with their heavy equipment.

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:14.319
So his first choice is, are we going to keep
on? Are we going to fight this thing, by ourselves?

00:05:14.319 --> 00:05:22.509
Really? That's not a choice that in politics
as in life, by the way, you can just say,

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:26.721
“Well, I think this is best or I think that
is best.” Whatever you come up with you’re

00:05:26.721 --> 00:05:32.569
going to have to fight for it, because in
the British political system as in the American

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:38.680
at least before Obama, there's no one person
who just gets to make up his mind and that's

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:45.729
it.
Sure enough about 10 days after Churchill

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:51.469
became Prime Minister they start hearing from
Mussolini who's not in the war yet. He says,

00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:55.770
“We could have a peace conference, and Italy
would agree to host the peace conference and

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:01.500
put it together.” Of course they want something
for that. Who knows? Maybe Gibraltar, maybe

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:11.190
something in Africa. The thing is they’re
going to be overwhelmed. Sure enough the foreign

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.789
minister, Edward Halifax likes the idea of
this thing.

00:06:14.789 --> 00:06:23.879
You have to stop a minute and think everything
that happened after made the 28th, 1940 when

00:06:23.879 --> 00:06:28.940
this came to a head, everyone who died who
was British and everyone who was killed by

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:34.490
a British person, and you could also say by
extension everyone who died who was an American

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:44.259
or who was killed by an American. That history
stems from the choice they made on that day.

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:49.400
Because they might have started their peace
conference and then Britain would not be invaded

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:56.529
or bombed as it was. It was almost invaded
and it was bombed. Churchill is in a place

00:06:56.529 --> 00:07:00.150
where he's got to take responsibility for
that.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:03.789
Churchill by the way is desperately afraid
of war all his life. I'll tell you about that

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:12.050
in a minute. You've got to think, we're getting
creamed here. If there's a way not to go through

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:17.589
all this, wouldn't that be better? That's
what Edward Halifax thought. He was a serious

00:07:17.589 --> 00:07:26.659
man, a good man. Wrong I think sometimes in
more important ways, but good. So what’s

00:07:26.659 --> 00:07:33.949
he to do?
You remember that in the summer of 1940 he

00:07:33.949 --> 00:07:38.710
made a speech that ends with, “If the British
Empire should last a thousand years, men will

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:43.749
still say this was their finest hour.” It’s
a beautiful thing to write. He wrote that

00:07:43.749 --> 00:07:48.270
himself and many other very beautiful things.
If you ask the question, by the way, who's

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:55.180
waiting in the wings who’s like Winston
Churchill? Read and listen, because boy, he

00:07:55.180 --> 00:08:01.969
could write and he could speak. He really
could. The people today mostly it's a different

00:08:01.969 --> 00:08:06.629
kind of thing. Although some of them are getting
better.

00:08:06.629 --> 00:08:12.190
Here's what he did on the 28th of May, 1940.
The war cabinet seems divided. There are five

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:18.869
people in it. Halifax seems like he wants
to make some kind of a deal, and they've been

00:08:18.869 --> 00:08:23.300
arguing about it for a few days. But they
don't bring it to conclusion because if Churchill

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:30.029
said, “I'm adamant that we won't do this,”
and then Halifax resigned, which he might,

00:08:30.029 --> 00:08:35.250
a point of principle, a big thing, and that's
the style in Britain, then probably the government

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:41.500
would fall and then Churchill would be out
of power. So he couldn't order them.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.430
What he did was there was a cabinet meeting.
He got a cabinet meeting together that day.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:50.470
That's a bigger body. There were five in the
war cabinet and they were 40 or so in the

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:55.330
cabinet. I don’t actually know the number
of that. Richard Langworth does. He knows

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.090
all those numbers.
Andrew: 28.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.770
Larry: 28?
Andrew: My name’s Andrew.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:06.340
Larry: Oh Andrew is sitting right there. Yeah,
so Andrew there were 28. As I was saying there

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:16.280
were 28. One of Churchill's favorite sayings
was if you keep a dog you don't have to bark

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:26.720
yourself. I 
got my two yappers sitting right down there.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:33.210
We're going to do fine in this speech today.
So they're 28. He talks about an hour. The

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:37.490
best notes are by a man named Hugh Dalton.
He was a labor minister and very left and

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:45.210
also liked Churchill, always did. Here's how
Churchill ended. First of all he draws a picture

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:49.450
of the war. He says, “The French are going
to quit. We're going to get thrown off. We

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:54.370
don't know what we're going to get back. Then
there's going to be a battle in the Channel.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:10:00.550
We have a navy. Hard to beat that navy, but
on the other hand air power is probably going

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:05.000
to decide,” and they had more airplanes.
On the other hand, third-hand, “Most of

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.950
the battles would be over Britain and we'll
have an advantage. We've done pretty well

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:15.300
in the initial air battle, so we got to fight
it out,” he says.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:20.710
This is what the thing is going to look like.
It's very bleak. Nobody's saying we're going

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:24.700
to maneuver over here and go there and then
get to Berlin. They're saying how are we going

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:31.530
to survive. Then he says, “I've been thinking
in these last few days whether it is part

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:35.550
of my duty to open negotiations with that
man.” He doesn’t mention that there's

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:41.500
a definite proposal to do that in front the
war cabinet. “And I believe that if I were

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:45.770
for a moment to consider parlay or surrender,
every one of you would rise up and tear me

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:52.040
down from my place. If this island story is
to end at last, let it end when each of us

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:58.790
lies choking in his own blood upon the ground.”
Now by the way, that’s a heck of a thing

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:06.410
to say. There was a demonstration, there was
wild applause, and Halifax thought maybe when

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:10.680
they resumed the war cabinet that they would
be on a slippery slope if they were to begin

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:15.180
these negotiations.
Now what I want you to understand is two things.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:21.570
One is in principle that choice is just like
the choice you make whether you go to the

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:27.760
concert or the ball game on the one hand or
do your job on the other. It's a choice between

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:36.550
competing goods. The second one is Churchill
was the kind of guy who put himself in the

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:43.490
place to make choices like that and thought
he was made for such choices.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:49.000
That choice is extremely consequential, because
there's a conservative-leaning historian name

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:55.971
Charmley. Is he still around Andrew? You see
him? Yeah. I don't like him very much, but

00:11:55.971 --> 00:12:04.230
he's pretty smart. He wrote a book in which
he blames the decline of Britain on that choice

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:12.530
that day and says that they should’ve made
it different. Churchill himself when he says,

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:17.570
“If the British Empire should last a thousand
years, men will still say this was their finest

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:27.000
hour,” he's trading the thousand years for
that day. That's a serious choice. Did he

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:38.350
make it right? That's a question. Just like
all of your choices, that's a question.

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:45.230
Now that led when he introduced this funny
thing about statesmanship and constitutionalism,

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:51.340
because they’re opposites, aren’t they?
At the time Churchill reached his glory because

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:55.510
he became the greatest man in the world, and
he was treated that way, although people voted

00:12:55.510 --> 00:13:03.590
against his party in 1945. Everybody treated
him for the rest of his life like wow what

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:15.620
a man and he'd never had that before.
At that time the great world statesmen were

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:23.520
of course the ones you know. They were Adolf
Hitler and Joseph Stalin and Franklin Roosevelt.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:31.790
What were their opinions about constitutions?
They had the statesmen’s opinion about constitutions.

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:41.360
All of them thought, “Heck, there's a lot
to do. We can't be concerned with old rules

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:48.291
that are made a long time ago before our circumstances.”
Constitution, that's what it's like. Every

00:13:48.291 --> 00:13:54.820
law is supposed to be a general statement,
made in advance, applying to all, and easy

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:59.840
to understand, made before you know who's
going to be hurt by it. We don't make laws

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:05.670
that way. We make rules now that actually
name, by name often who's going to be benefited

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:11.310
and who's goings to be harmed. But the rule
of law means that laws are made in general

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:16.840
and in advance and without respecting the
persons who’ll be affected.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:23.240
Constitutions are the biggest kinds of laws.
A person who's good at making choices like

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:29.500
that, because what Churchill did on the 28th
of May 1940 was no normal thing. Because the

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:37.570
whole world is falling apart around his ears
and he's wanted that job. He said so since

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:45.260
he was in his 20s. Now at last he's got it
and this is what he has to decide. He has

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:55.170
written, I will tell you. He has written this
in 1925, “Mankind has never been in this

00:14:55.170 --> 00:15:01.680
position before. Without improving appreciably
in virtue or enjoying wiser guidance, he has

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:09.050
got into his hands at last the means of his
own destruction. Death stands waiting at attention,

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:16.291
silent expectant, waiting for a word from
the frail being, so long his servant, now

00:15:16.291 --> 00:15:21.561
for once is master.”
That's what Churchill thought about war. He

00:15:21.561 --> 00:15:25.680
thought that about war since he saw the Battle
of Omdurman in 1898 the Charles Krauthammer

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.350
mentioned last night. He was horrified by
that battle and he thought, “Gracious sakes,”

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:36.570
in this war where he won his glory, the Second
World War he’s asking in the middle of it,

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:40.020
“What are we going to call this one war,”
and he said, “That's easy, this is the unnecessary

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:48.520
war. This should have been prevented. I tried
to prevent it.” He doesn't want this thing,

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:55.570
and yet, all these other people who have failed
to prepare for fear of war in part, that's

00:15:55.570 --> 00:16:00.350
what Stanley Baldwin said, they're not as
eloquent as Churchill about the dangers of

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:05.860
war. Now the war is upon them and some of
those same people are thinking it's time to

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:12.120
quit.
You can't write a constitution that takes

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:17.240
account of those circumstances. What you need
to take account of out those circumstances

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:23.940
is some extraordinary human being. What he
did see it wasn't enough because it was like

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:31.750
a very grand political maneuver that he pulled
off. Because these guys, the people are Neville

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:40.930
Chamberlain, Edward Halifax, Arthur Greenwood,
Clement Attlee, those two are socialists in

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:44.940
much in the minority in the House of Commons
and no friends of Churchill's except during

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:52.200
the war they were, and Churchill. These two
conservatives, they control the votes. If

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:58.050
they leave the government's gone. What he
does is go to a wider audience and make them

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:03.300
a speech and a heck of a speech, which by
the way is a rare important one that he didn’t

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:11.720
write out in advance. He's responsible for
that. You couldn't decide that by constitutions.

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:20.419
James Madison says about our Constitution,
he says, “Enlightened statesmen will not

00:17:20.419 --> 00:17:27.339
always be at the helm,” and so we're going
to need grand laws that keep things steady

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:35.350
and encourage good behavior, inform our characters.
Madison then is like Churchill because Madison

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:40.759
was an inventor of a nation along with his
friends who wrote the Constitution. They wrote

00:17:40.759 --> 00:17:47.369
a constitution in which they did not get latitude
to do whatever they wanted.

00:17:47.369 --> 00:17:56.539
Whereas Hitler, do you know how Hitler came
to power? In about 1910 he was a street bum,

00:17:56.539 --> 00:18:03.400
living rough. He learned to make speeches
ranting around open fires in the street about

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:10.509
the Jews, didn't like those Jews. But by 1925
he's been in the army and the army is behind

00:18:10.509 --> 00:18:17.130
him now in politics and he's becoming an important
guy. He never rises above the rank of Corporal.

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:26.070
Then in 1931 and 32 and 33 is when this happened,
he gets in front of General Hindenburg, the

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:31.340
great hero, the senior German military officer
of modern times and now the president the

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:38.790
Weimar Republic. Hindenburg says, “We want
to invite you to form a government.” Hitler,

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:46.889
little corporate Hitler says, “Can't do
it unless you agree that we can pass a law

00:18:46.889 --> 00:18:56.909
that my cabinet can do anything it wants to
do.” There must be no constitution. Of course,

00:18:56.909 --> 00:19:03.590
why, because he says, “This is an emergency
and I must be allowed to do whatever I want.”

00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:09.940
Churchill is the same kind of man, because
if you look on May 28th, 1940, what did he

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:17.909
do? I mean it is stunning what he did, and
he is responsible for that thing, and he wanted

00:19:17.909 --> 00:19:22.629
to be. It wasn't enough. If he'd sat there
like the rest of them wringing his hands and

00:19:22.629 --> 00:19:26.399
saying, “Well, it's this thing or that and
the war could be very bad and all that stuff.”

00:19:26.399 --> 00:19:32.830
He didn't. He got a bunch of people at Rome
and he told them, “It might well mean the

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:38.220
death of everyone sitting here, but we'd better
do this thing.”

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:46.820
If you want to why, he says that in the Munich
speech. It’s by the way a primer in political

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:51.690
science. The whole speech is one of the greatest
political speeches ever given in life, in

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:57.970
history. First of all he says, “What I cannot
bear is the sense of us following under the

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:03.490
orbit and the influence of these Nazis.”
Then toward the end of the speech he says,

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:08.159
“You,” and this is Aristotle’s way of
thinking, he says, “You must consider the

00:20:08.159 --> 00:20:13.490
character of the Nazi regime and the rule
which it implies. What kind of people are

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:22.140
they? What do they do? Doesn’t by the way
the world cry out right now for some statesmen

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:29.990
to say that these Iranians and these Chinese
are brutal rulers, and if you disagree with

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:36.149
them they will put you in the pokey for doing
that or they will cut off your head? That

00:20:36.149 --> 00:20:42.950
is the character of the regime and the rule
that they imply. They are arming.” Churchill

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:49.470
says in 1936, “There are really only two
facts, Germany is Hitler's and Germany is

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:58.820
arming.”
A man able to do things like that. In politics

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:05.929
he was just the same way as he was on a battlefield.
He was a very lethal human being. He kept

00:21:05.929 --> 00:21:12.299
his nerve. He could think. That's what's most
dangerous. People get excited and they crash

00:21:12.299 --> 00:21:18.860
around and they charge, “Mad” Anthony
Wayne in the bat- He was wounded one time.

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:23.610
Anthony Wayne, a general in the American Revolutionary
Army, Wayne County, Detroit is named after

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.690
him. He’s a very great man. What did he
do?

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:30.210
He got all head up about the British, he was
fighting them, and he's riding around and

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:34.370
he gets up against one of their bunkers and
he can't contain himself. He lift off his

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:40.919
horse over the bunker in amongst the British
and they stabbed him. It was a stupid thing

00:21:40.919 --> 00:21:47.669
to do. He was angry. He thought George Washington
was the bravest man he ever saw, because George

00:21:47.669 --> 00:21:53.909
Washington was thinking when he was fighting.
That by the way is the meaning of the term

00:21:53.909 --> 00:22:00.789
courage. It is not your fear which you must
feel that tells you what to do. It is your

00:22:00.789 --> 00:22:06.289
noggin which is still operating. That's what
kind of a guy he was.

00:22:06.289 --> 00:22:15.630
So why in 1936 would this guy write an article
about American politics? The article is called

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:20.700
“What good’s the Constitution” and it's
about the court packing scheme. It’s published

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:28.940
in 1935 in Britain and in 1936 in America.
What he says is the Supreme Court has struck

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:35.690
down the National Recovery Act, which by the
way gave the White House and its agency power

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:42.419
to set wages and prices and allocate goods
everywhere in the society. He says, “We

00:22:42.419 --> 00:22:46.399
British look at that is kind of odd because
there isn't any court that can do that,”

00:22:46.399 --> 00:22:53.769
but he says, “I,” and see Franklin Roosevelt,
what did he say, he, I hasten to say Churchill

00:22:53.769 --> 00:22:59.120
admired Roosevelt and he called him the greatest
foreign friend of Britain that Britain ever

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:06.669
hand, but here in 1936 he's taking issue with
him. He says, “The difference between constitutional

00:23:06.669 --> 00:23:15.990
rule and not having it is the difference between,”
this is a favorite formulation of his, “between

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:22.299
nations which own peoples and peoples which
own nations. Or government which own peoples

00:23:22.299 --> 00:23:32.440
and peoples who own governments.”
That's curious. What I think about him then

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:44.369
is that he loved to rule and he wished to
rule under law. In these hard times you need

00:23:44.369 --> 00:23:55.190
somebody lethal. Ronald Reagan was a lethal
guy. He was an actor. What would you think

00:23:55.190 --> 00:24:01.659
right? But I know the man who took the Prime
Minister of Bermuda in to see Ronald Reagan

00:24:01.659 --> 00:24:08.669
that led to the Granada and an invasion. Eugenia
Charles was her name and the ambassador William

00:24:08.669 --> 00:24:14.789
Middendorf, friend of mine today. Middendorf
here's her story and he says, “I'm going

00:24:14.789 --> 00:24:20.320
to take you to the White House.” They get
in and they sit and Reagan doesn't say very

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:28.629
much. He had an executive temper. He didn't
blather on. He didn't argue with all the people

00:24:28.629 --> 00:24:33.120
who right up to the minute he got out of the
limousine told him not to say the words tear

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:38.910
down this wall. He never argued with any of
them. It's just he was getting ready to be

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:43.049
standing up there giving a speech and he could
say whatever he pleased.

00:24:43.049 --> 00:24:48.610
George Schultz put somebody in the limo with
him to urge him at the last minute not to

00:24:48.610 --> 00:24:55.249
say that. You see, with this thing, Middendorf
tells me, he didn't make a bunch of speeches.

00:24:55.249 --> 00:25:02.859
What he said was, “Okay, I understand.”
He said, “I'll be back to you tomorrow about

00:25:02.859 --> 00:25:11.610
this.” 24 hours later we were on our way
there. That’s the executive temper. It’s

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:21.559
the opposite of the law temper in so many
ways. You need both.

00:25:21.559 --> 00:25:26.820
It's like the virtue of prudence, isn’t
it? Because when you choose all the time … By

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:32.460
the way, don't you doubt your choices all
the time? I have a job where I make a million

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:40.270
of them. I’m blessed with my friends I work
with, because I can see what's right in their

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:44.750
faces. Sometimes Wayland says this about me,
sometimes I'll be talking and what I want

00:25:44.750 --> 00:25:49.910
them to do is smile or not because then I’ll
know I'm going down the right road. If they

00:25:49.910 --> 00:25:56.919
don't … Churchill had friends like that,
right? But when you decide and commit yourself

00:25:56.919 --> 00:26:04.279
to action, that's the other part of prudence.
There's something in you that doubts, something

00:26:04.279 --> 00:26:10.359
that's different from you, something that's
different from what you do that sits in judgment

00:26:10.359 --> 00:26:18.820
upon what you do, in your own soul and in
the souls of others. You have to listen that.

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:26.629
Envy in the executive branch, lethal in action.
That means by the way the relationship between

00:26:26.629 --> 00:26:32.730
the particular actions of a statesman and
a constitution is like the relationship inside

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:40.539
the soul that takes account of all the circumstances
that play, and also takes account of something

00:26:40.539 --> 00:26:49.570
ultimate that's quite beyond you. When you're
dealing with somebody like Adolf Hitler, because

00:26:49.570 --> 00:26:56.129
that guy needs killing, and it's going cost
millions of lives to kill him unless you do

00:26:56.129 --> 00:27:04.590
it first and early. Churchill had so much
to do with his killing. Yet, because Churchill

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:12.950
heard that voice that was not the same thing
as his will, he was not in need of that killing.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:26.279
I want to say that I think Winston Churchill
was a humble man. I mean Mr. Singer was really

00:27:26.279 --> 00:27:34.210
good and he conveyed something of Churchill's
amazing cockiness. You’re a worm. Yes. We're

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:44.610
all worms. Yes, but I'm glowworm. But you
don't find Churchill standing up giving long

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:55.859
speeches about himself. He writes about things
he does a lot. The first person personal pronoun

00:27:55.859 --> 00:28:03.570
is uncommon and hardly ever directly praised.
When he’s dealing with Neville Chamberlain

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:07.690
later which he dealt with him, one of the
most beautiful things he ever wrote is a eulogy

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:15.179
to Neville Chamberlain. Neville Chamberlain
died in 1940, not long after he lost the Premiership.

00:28:15.179 --> 00:28:19.980
Churchill admired him and respected him, and
he was generous with him. Churchill didn't

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:27.490
count coup over him and his kind in any obvious
way. He says, “We got to write that off.

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:32.690
We all have reason to search our speeches
and see when we've been right. Now we're one

00:28:32.690 --> 00:28:44.580
country and we're going to fight this guy.”
I want to say a word about the Empire. Churchill

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:54.590
was an imperialist and India was the big question.
Lots of good people think that Churchill was

00:28:54.590 --> 00:29:00.889
foolish to fight against India in the early
1930s because it lost him credibility against

00:29:00.889 --> 00:29:06.979
Hitler. Richard Langworth hosts debates about
that all the time. I'm a partisan in those

00:29:06.979 --> 00:29:11.479
debates.
But here's what I think. I think Churchill

00:29:11.479 --> 00:29:20.789
thought that the right has got to become very
mighty in the modern world in order to defend

00:29:20.789 --> 00:29:27.780
itself. I think he thought that Britain was
in a providential place about that. Because

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:34.850
you know especially the Indian part of the
British Empire, and the big ones are Australia,

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:45.129
Canada, New Zealand, South Africa, what am
I leaving out? West Indies. And those big

00:29:45.129 --> 00:29:54.320
ones were really governing themselves by early
in Churchill's day. But India was different.

00:29:54.320 --> 00:30:03.190
The British got it by happenstance. India
had a million and a half men under arms on

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:07.539
the British and American side in the First
World War and two and a half million in the

00:30:07.539 --> 00:30:15.519
Second World War. It made Britain great, big.
Their casualty rates in the Second World War

00:30:15.519 --> 00:30:21.500
were significantly higher than the British,
and they were volunteers by the way. Britain

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:26.450
didn't have the power to make them do it.
In India they were ruling India, but the Indian

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:34.350
Army was entirely volunteer. Churchill thought
we're going to have to be a great power to

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:42.391
defend ourselves and this helps.
But that, you see, because by the way, if

00:30:42.391 --> 00:30:46.690
all they are is like the Spartans and the
Athenians, just telling them what to do and

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:53.840
ruling them by force, what becomes of Churchill's
vaunted and oft-repeated claims that everyone

00:30:53.840 --> 00:31:01.889
has a right to govern himself, that the petition
of right and the Magna Carta and the petition

00:31:01.889 --> 00:31:05.950
and the …. Sorry, I got to get my order
right, the Magna Carta and the English Bill

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:11.990
of Rights and the American Declaration of
Independence are the great title deeds of

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:21.399
the rights of man. What comes of that if they’re
just using India so they can be strong?

00:31:21.399 --> 00:31:28.009
China is all wrapped up in the Middle East
right now, and we know what China governs.

00:31:28.009 --> 00:31:36.059
Five years ago they published their … every
seven or 10 year major paper on the meaning

00:31:36.059 --> 00:31:41.120
of democracy and what were their identities.
We have that now, democracy means that the

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:46.849
party decides in the interest to the people,
and then we can steer the society to some

00:31:46.849 --> 00:31:52.940
perfection later.
What will they do with their imperial influence

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:59.299
in the Middle East? How will they treat those
people? What advantages are they going to

00:31:59.299 --> 00:32:06.450
get from it? Because they’re spending a
lot of money there now. Why is Iran occupying

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:11.619
five capitals right now in the Middle East?
What are they going to get from that? What

00:32:11.619 --> 00:32:15.700
are they going to do with that? You can tell
if you look at the way they treat their own

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:22.619
people. Is Britain like that? Is that what
they were guilty of, as was claimed by many

00:32:22.619 --> 00:32:29.570
people and is claimed by many historians today?
Here's what he said. It's interesting what

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:37.190
he said you see because that part of the world
matters a lot and a lot it's going on there,

00:32:37.190 --> 00:32:44.349
and between here and there in the Middle East
and power is at stake. Churchill said, “We

00:32:44.349 --> 00:32:50.669
got India in a massive act of neglect.”
It actually was conquered by a man named Clive

00:32:50.669 --> 00:32:59.511
who was a clerk in a British East India Company.
Then it was given to the Queen. Now we've

00:32:59.511 --> 00:33:06.090
got it, and we've been good for it, and the
Indians are accepting of our rule. We don't

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:13.379
have to shoot people. In the one big instance
where they did Churchill condemned the general.

00:33:13.379 --> 00:33:18.070
We don't have many people over there. It's
very good for them and we've established the

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:25.129
rule of law. That means they're on their way
to self-government. We get things for it that

00:33:25.129 --> 00:33:30.820
are really great. It would be wrong to think
of the Empire as just good for those who are

00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:35.129
governed. It can be good for the mother country
too, as long as it's good for both.

00:33:35.129 --> 00:33:43.119
Then he says, “Their population has quadrupled,
but there are three things about it that mean

00:33:43.119 --> 00:33:50.830
that if we give them self-rule there’s going
to be hundreds of thousands or millions killed.

00:33:50.830 --> 00:33:56.710
One of them it's a whole collection of lots
of different peoples who don't speak the same

00:33:56.710 --> 00:34:04.110
language. One of them is those two are broadly
divided into Muslim and Hindu. If we leave

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:15.480
they're going to kill each other. The third
one is in Hinduism there's the caste system,

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:21.100
untouchables at the bottom, the Dalits.”
He says, “If we leave that's going to get

00:34:21.100 --> 00:34:24.530
worse.”
By the way there's a really good book written

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:30.060
by a journalist, an Indian man who lives in
England now called “Modern India.” He

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:35.280
says in there that there's data that shows
that for the first 15 years of home rule in

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:42.500
India after the Second World War the caste
system was solidified and people were for

00:34:42.500 --> 00:34:48.460
it. Churchill says, “We are responsible
for all this, and if we leave we’ll be responsible

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:54.460
for the deaths.” And the deaths are estimated
700,000 to a million when they left. Of course

00:34:54.460 --> 00:34:59.080
the Muslims and the Hindus split from each
other in Pakistan and India, and that's part

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:05.660
of the problem in the world right now. He
said, “We should stay and be confident in

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:10.010
our principles of freedom.”
I'm not telling you that because I think he

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:16.080
was right about that. It's a long time now.
India by the way is possibly a massive and

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:23.460
wonderful ally, and thank God for their direction,
and Mr. Modi. But what I want you to know

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:28.770
is Churchill never doubted their capacity
for self-government and never said they didn't

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:37.170
have it. Even often asserted that they did.
There was a plan for the world which he thought

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:45.000
Britain and the United States were obliged
to make because of the goodness of their principles

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:51.220
which had given rise to their strength. Sure
enough we’re governed today by people who

00:35:51.220 --> 00:36:03.080
doubt the goodness of our principles and our
country and are dissipating our strength.

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:09.790
My last points about domestic policy. I think
Churchill thought that the things that had

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:15.581
made war more dangerous were the same things
that made politics more dangerous than they

00:36:15.581 --> 00:36:22.400
had been before. Both had always been very
dangerous. No more he thought. What were those

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:29.560
things? War had been transformed he said by
science. That is to say we can just make weapons

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:34.760
to slaughter. It’s getting cheap to kill
people. Our societies are much richer and

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:40.910
they can afford to slaughter each other for
longer. They used to run out of food in the

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:44.310
earlier wars and they couldn't campaign in
the winter, because they couldn’t get men

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:48.220
around and they couldn’t sustain them in
the field. Now we can fight all the time and

00:36:48.220 --> 00:36:54.140
for years. We have these amazing weapons and
they're getting stronger and stronger.

00:36:54.140 --> 00:37:00.440
But these weapons which are so powerful, like
this thing, this is my iPhone. I never go

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:06.320
anywhere without it. It's liberating and makes
it easier for me to run the college and stay

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:14.640
in touch, make the kids stop being naughty
even from a distance. That power, we love

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:20.250
that power, and it makes us healthier, and
it makes us richer, and it makes our lives

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:25.420
more comfortable. We can save people that
might have died earlier, our loved ones.

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:32.020
But now the idea Churchill said is that we
should apply that power to the governing of

00:37:32.020 --> 00:37:39.270
other human beings. We should take this science
that is transforming everywhere everything

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:48.680
and we should use it to plan how we govern
people because we could perfect the society.

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:54.720
Churchill publishes a novel in 1898 and in
the novel there's a communist name of Karl

00:37:54.720 --> 00:38:03.470
with a K. It's a cool … I love the novel
myself. I also think it's one of Churchill’s

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:12.150
greatest autobiographical works. He's in it
by the name of Savrola. There’s a beautiful

00:38:12.150 --> 00:38:18.170
woman named Lucile which means light and she's
the wife of the despot who rules the place.

00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:23.930
Savrola at the end of the novel gets the state
and he gets Lucile, he gets the girl.

00:38:23.930 --> 00:38:31.320
You see what that means? That means she is
a symbol of the state. The way she's treated

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:38.580
by three people who are two quarters show
how everybody will be treated depending on

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:45.780
who rules the state. Her husband Molara the
despot they have a discussion about beauty.

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:51.220
By the way Churchill is just a kid and he
writes this stuff. It’s extremely interesting

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:57.820
to me. I think he knew what he was doing.
Molara, the despot says, “Beauty is only

00:38:57.820 --> 00:39:05.550
fashion, it's just whatever we think it is,
it changes from time to time.” If you think

00:39:05.550 --> 00:39:11.390
about that for a minute, if you think you
have the power to define what is beautiful,

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:16.420
which means by the way also by extension the
lower think what is good, then you're a very

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:21.580
powerful man. Molara, the despot is interested
in power.

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:29.050
Now her other suitor is Karl, the socialist.
His buddy says, “What does the Savrola know

00:39:29.050 --> 00:39:34.460
of the community of property,” and his friend
unnamed says, “Or the community of wives.

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:42.320
I'd like to have the president's wife.”
She overhears that and shudders. Now do you

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:49.300
think that's a serious point? It might be
because the family is a terrible source of

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:56.410
inequality. There’s a young woman that lives
in Austin, Texas. She is brilliant and she

00:39:56.410 --> 00:40:03.490
is beautiful. I commonly refer to her as the
most important young academic in America today.

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:07.980
That's kind of an insult to people because
I employ a whole bunch of young academics

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:15.000
at Hillsdale College, but none of them is
named Kathleen Arnn, and none of them is as

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:21.140
good looking as she is, and none of them is
as fine as she is. By the way she is mine

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:26.530
and I favor her. It would be wrong if I took
college resources to favor her, and I don't

00:40:26.530 --> 00:40:34.450
do that, but I wish I could.
You see how that makes things unequal, because

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:40.340
whatever I am is at her disposal. She's the
headmaster of a charter school. They're eight

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:45.130
of them now and they’re soon going to be
13. Which one of them can call me any night

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:50.140
of the week and get the best advice I've got
to give? I know something about that kind

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:59.730
of thing. So if you're going make everything
really equal by science Churchill is saying

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:06.530
and he later says this explicitly in his speeches,
then what you would do is you would get rid

00:41:06.530 --> 00:41:11.050
of the family.
Molara says, “Beauty is just whatever we

00:41:11.050 --> 00:41:17.910
make of it,” so she can have the socialist
for a husband and then she's going to be everybody's

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:25.170
property, or she can have her husband for
a husband and he says, “Beauty is just whatever

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:30.600
I say it is.” She asked him, “Really?
There's no standard of beauty outside you?”

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:35.540
She asked him that and he insults her because
she's a beautiful woman by saying, “No,

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:40.620
none whatsoever.” Do you see what he took
from her? That's how she's going to live if

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:46.430
she stays married to him.
Savrola says, “Yes, fashion is a thing and

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:52.930
it changes all the time, but look at that
statue,” and he points to a replica of a

00:41:52.930 --> 00:42:02.670
Greek statue and he says, “Men have called
that beautiful for 2000 years.” That means

00:42:02.670 --> 00:42:07.880
Savrola does not claim that he can say what
is beautiful.

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:15.750
What if it's true that we teach our kids today
in school that anything they think it's true

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:23.960
is true because the standards of truth are
shifting all the time? On what ground then

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:33.510
would you say to any power that your use of
that power is wrongful? I'm saying Winston

00:42:33.510 --> 00:42:42.870
Churchill figured that out when he was a boy.
I think the knowledge of that is why he was

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:48.920
able to kill Hitler. I think that's why he
saw socialism for what it was from the first

00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:56.880
minute he looked at it.
In 1931 he says carrying on this theme about

00:42:56.880 --> 00:43:05.290
the family. By the way, have you read what's
happening to the family in the world? Let's

00:43:05.290 --> 00:43:11.470
say we're all important people. We’re here.
I'm an important guy, right? I'm going to

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:20.600
get old and die. Whom am I going to want around
me? What is my real domain? My family and

00:43:20.600 --> 00:43:28.810
my friends. I happen to have a lot of friends,
I’m lucky about that. That means honors

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:34.830
and stations and things like that. Those are
important, those matter. But what matters,

00:43:34.830 --> 00:43:39.650
did you hear somebody asked Barry Singer about
Churchill's children. Churchill was heartbroken

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:46.250
about his children, many times, and he loved
them very much. He did spoil them.

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:55.390
In 1931 he said, “You know, we're going
to get the power to make babies the way we

00:43:55.390 --> 00:44:02.150
want them. And when we do, we're going to
make some of them better and we’re going

00:44:02.150 --> 00:44:08.150
to make some of them worse. The worse ones
will use to work for us. And we’ll breed

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:12.700
them so they won't mind, and we’ll do this
in laboratories.” He wrote this in the same

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:17.210
year that Brave New World was published, although
it looks like he only read it in 1958. He

00:44:17.210 --> 00:44:24.180
wrote that to his wife in that year.
You see what's possible if you just take science,

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:30.860
if you redefine science away from to know
and to control, which is what it means in

00:44:30.860 --> 00:44:37.940
many uses today, and you make that sovereign.
Then why can't you just remake humanity into

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:44.620
whatever you want? That's what Hitler wanted.
That's what Stalin wanted.

00:44:44.620 --> 00:44:49.190
In The Wall Street Journal two weeks ago two
Nobel prize-winning biochemists published

00:44:49.190 --> 00:44:57.700
an article calling for a moratorium on changing
what's called the germline in DNA. That's

00:44:57.700 --> 00:45:04.161
the line that is transmitted from one generation
to the next. The minute we alter it, and there

00:45:04.161 --> 00:45:11.490
are things that we can do to it now that will
remove at least one hereditary disease. But

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:19.280
he said the minute, these two scientists,
“The minute we alter it, then it becomes

00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:27.110
an artifact, it becomes something we have
made.” I'm using my terms now. Then what

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:32.310
are the limits on how we alter it? In other
words, this thing that Churchill says since

00:45:32.310 --> 00:45:41.060
1931 is going to happen, that's a practical
issue alive today.

00:45:41.060 --> 00:45:47.580
What I think is amazing about Winston Churchill
is how much he could see and how well he could

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:55.880
explain it. Coolidge said that great statesmen
are the ambassadors of providence sent to

00:45:55.880 --> 00:46:05.650
reveal to us our unknowns selves. I think
Churchill is such a messenger, and I think

00:46:05.650 --> 00:46:25.870
we should study him. Thank you.
Does anybody want to say anything? Yes sir.

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:40.010
Hi Ross.
Ross: I’ll tell you what. I’ll make this

00:46:40.010 --> 00:46:44.320
very short so you could go longer. I'll make
a statement. You tell me why I'm right or

00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:51.630
wrong. Comparisons between Barack Obama and
Neville Chamberlain are unfair to Neville

00:46:51.630 --> 00:47:02.030
Chamberlain. Tell me if I'm right or wrong
and why?

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:13.670
Larry: Chamberlain's personal habits were
humble and self-restraint. I think I am interested

00:47:13.670 --> 00:47:18.750
what Richard and Andrew think about this because
they know a lot about it. I think Churchill

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:25.920
actually disliked Stanley Baldwin, although
he found him witty and charming. Chamberlain

00:47:25.920 --> 00:47:33.090
stiffened up when he was around Churchill,
but at the end they became warm to each other.

00:47:33.090 --> 00:47:39.140
Chamberlain did something very foolish. The
way Churchill explained it was he was deceived

00:47:39.140 --> 00:47:44.310
and misguided in his greatest hopes by a wicked
man.

00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:54.200
I don't think Obama like Dick Cheney said
last last week something that I think Krauthammer

00:47:54.200 --> 00:48:00.950
hinted at last night but he didn't say it.
I’ll say what I thought he meant but mark

00:48:00.950 --> 00:48:08.090
it down that I don't know that this is what
he meant. I think what Cheney said was it's

00:48:08.090 --> 00:48:16.440
possible that Obama means harm to our country.
I do think it's true he doesn't like the kind

00:48:16.440 --> 00:48:24.360
of thing it has been. It's his own statement
that he means to transform it and thinks that

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:29.450
Lincoln and Reagan are great transformers,
and he's going to transform them. That means

00:48:29.450 --> 00:48:35.550
he's going to undo what they did, and therefore
be like them. I think that's his model. I

00:48:35.550 --> 00:48:41.740
think that model is like Molara, the tyrant
in Savrola’s model. He's going to make things

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:44.510
the way they want them and he’s going to
make them different so everybody can know

00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:53.080
that it's the way they want them.
Harry Jaffa used to say, the great man, he

00:48:53.080 --> 00:49:00.400
used to say that God made a promise to the
Jews after the flood that he would not let

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:07.530
them be destroyed. That's why Hitler wanted
to kill them, just to show who's the boss

00:49:07.530 --> 00:49:17.920
here now. I worry about that kind of thing.
It's hard for me to think that a man could

00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:23.230
take the office of President of United States
and mean harm to the country. I guess I don't

00:49:23.230 --> 00:49:29.310
assert that for myself right now. But I wonder
about it, and I do think that he doesn't like

00:49:29.310 --> 00:49:34.470
the kind of thing it has been. That's clear
enough.

00:49:34.470 --> 00:49:43.250
Speaker 5: Could you clarify Churchill's relationship
to Gandhi? Did he really dislike him and say

00:49:43.250 --> 00:49:50.349
the things that are quoted about him, please?
Larry: Well he did call him half-naked fakir

00:49:50.349 --> 00:49:57.790
or faqir, striding up the steps of Government
House and he did dislike him. During the Second

00:49:57.790 --> 00:50:02.120
World War Gandhi went on hunger strike and
Churchill would sent memos saying, “Is he

00:50:02.120 --> 00:50:09.640
still alive? Did somebody sneak him food?
Try to find out.” He was pretty tough about

00:50:09.640 --> 00:50:19.410
that. What Churchill thought afterwards was
different because when he was in this fight

00:50:19.410 --> 00:50:24.910
here's what Churchill thought. Churchill thought
the British Empire implied an order of the

00:50:24.910 --> 00:50:31.500
world that would be safe and secure and that
it would be a major component of the future

00:50:31.500 --> 00:50:36.880
safety and security of the world, and that
it would be good for the Indian people. Churchill’s

00:50:36.880 --> 00:50:41.970
accusation made beautifully and often against
Gandhi and his friends is that they are upsetting

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:47.080
that.
Then Churchill got beat on that. In the course

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:51.320
of getting beat and as he was getting beat
Gandhi send a representative to Churchill,

00:50:51.320 --> 00:50:59.100
to London, he was in London. His name was
G. D. Birla, B-I-R-L-A and by the way a word

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:05.360
about the greatness of Martin Gilbert. We
have some of the letters that are important

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:12.630
between Birla and Gandhi about Churchill from
the Gandhi papers, not from the Churchill

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:18.230
papers. These are not letters to Churchill.
Martin Gilbert looked everywhere. That's why

00:51:18.230 --> 00:51:25.600
we know what I'm about to tell you. The man
was tireless. He was a machine of a good kind.

00:51:25.600 --> 00:51:35.620
“Although he is no friend of our cause Churchill
is a great friend of India, and he wishes

00:51:35.620 --> 00:51:42.140
us well, and we talked at length and then
he invited me to his home for a weekend and

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:47.370
we had a great time.” I produced that letter
in response to a historian named Jeff Jeffrey

00:51:47.370 --> 00:51:58.690
Best who published that Churchill could not
bear the company of people of color. Churchill

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:06.300
admired very much Gandhi's standing up for
the Muslims and the untouchables and proud

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:13.370
to see that and congratulated him for that.
You know Gandhi was killed by a Muslim extremist.

00:52:13.370 --> 00:52:18.030
So what their argument was about was that
Churchill thought that they were depriving

00:52:18.030 --> 00:52:28.071
the world and India of a great good. It was
very hard for India for a long time. Lots

00:52:28.071 --> 00:52:39.210
of death.
What was that? Did I say Muslim? I meant Hindu

00:52:39.210 --> 00:52:44.660
extremist. The Hindus and Gandhi was a Hindu
didn't like him trying to make up with the

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:49.790
Muslims. I think he was living among Muslims
at the time and he was shot by a Hindu for

00:52:49.790 --> 00:52:58.270
that. See, my boys are down there. I think
that’s what the argument was about.

00:52:58.270 --> 00:53:08.160
I think myself that Gandhi was a great man,
but misguided about those things. I think

00:53:08.160 --> 00:53:12.310
what was going on in retrospect one thinks,
what Andrew thinks about this because he's

00:53:12.310 --> 00:53:17.950
written a lot about this kind of thing. I
think that the disease that has affected intellectual

00:53:17.950 --> 00:53:23.390
classes in America beginning at about this
time and a bit before I think it was in the

00:53:23.390 --> 00:53:28.790
intellectual classes in Britain too about
things like the Empire. I think there was

00:53:28.790 --> 00:53:35.210
… They didn't have the spirit for it and
they were broken up some by the First World

00:53:35.210 --> 00:53:40.390
War as they might have been, and I think they
didn't, they didn't believe in the mission

00:53:40.390 --> 00:53:46.440
of Britain in the way Churchill did.
It's hard for anybody to write. Andrew is

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:52.790
about the only serious historian who writes
this way, taking seriously Churchill’s claims,

00:53:52.790 --> 00:53:59.320
because they're beyond the pale now for anybody
to believe them. You have to be Andrew or

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:05.280
the president of Hillsdale College or Jim
Mahler who's not a historian and a few others.

00:54:05.280 --> 00:54:12.420
What do the rest of them think? That was all
so long ago and different and he never did

00:54:12.420 --> 00:54:16.000
catch up to it.
Charles Krauthammer says in a really brilliant

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:21.760
article he wrote about Churchill that Churchill
was a man of the 19th century. Well that's

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:26.540
not what I think. I don't think Churchill
was a man of a century, although there were

00:54:26.540 --> 00:54:30.470
lots of things about the 19th century that
he liked. Yes sir.

00:54:30.470 --> 00:54:43.670
Speaker 6: Doctor Arnn, your first question
AKA the difference between Chamberlain and

00:54:43.670 --> 00:54:55.530
Obama I thought was extremely intriguing.
I wonder if you would comment on Churchill's

00:54:55.530 --> 00:55:05.080
view of colonialism as opposed to constitutionalism,
and tell me if you think maybe the difference

00:55:05.080 --> 00:55:18.310
between 
Chamberlain and Obama was that Chamberlain

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:28.770
was not a Muslim?
Larry: I don't think Obama is a Muslim, but

00:55:28.770 --> 00:55:37.010
I don't know. Remember, that's a question.
Like when you speculate … Obama has said

00:55:37.010 --> 00:55:41.970
that he is not and there is that sentence
where he said he was one time, and if the

00:55:41.970 --> 00:55:47.740
quote is accurate I think he said it as a
way to identify and show sympathy with people

00:55:47.740 --> 00:55:53.390
who are Muslims. But the main point about
that is it's a reason why it's hard for me

00:55:53.390 --> 00:55:59.450
to say that I think Obama means harm to the
country, because that's a statement about

00:55:59.450 --> 00:56:06.620
his intentions. But if you try to account
for the facts that you see that idea is beginning

00:56:06.620 --> 00:56:14.271
to suggest itself.
About colonialism and constitutionalism, so

00:56:14.271 --> 00:56:20.030
here's what I think about it. I've actually
written about this now and about to be published,

00:56:20.030 --> 00:56:27.170
but what I think is Churchill thought that
ability to govern oneself was coincident with

00:56:27.170 --> 00:56:34.260
humanity and had nothing to do with color,
that races weren’t qualified or disqualified

00:56:34.260 --> 00:56:38.880
to govern themselves. I can't find anywhere
where he said the contrary of that in many

00:56:38.880 --> 00:56:44.360
places where he said that. I checked with
my living encyclopedia, Richard Langworth

00:56:44.360 --> 00:56:48.820
about that, because he thought I was being
too generous at one point commenting on my

00:56:48.820 --> 00:56:55.440
book about Churchill and Empire and maybe
I still am. I don't know, I think I changed

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:59.910
it some. But I said, do you, can you find
any place where Churchill said that the black

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:05.070
ones or the browns ones or the yellow ones
or the white ones are unqualified to govern

00:57:05.070 --> 00:57:09.520
themselves. I mean I don't think there is
such a place.

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:19.850
What I think he did think was this. What's
going to happen if you have elections in Egypt

00:57:19.850 --> 00:57:26.890
or Palestine? First of all, they’re surrounded
by hostility and tyranny and much influenced

00:57:26.890 --> 00:57:34.830
by that. Second of all, they're divided among
themselves in powerful ways. I said to a senior

00:57:34.830 --> 00:57:39.770
official in the White House at an important
moment one time. He said, “Do you believe

00:57:39.770 --> 00:57:47.730
in freedom in Iraq,” and I said, “Yeah.”
He said, “But …” I said, “Well, isn't

00:57:47.730 --> 00:57:53.960
it difficult?” He said, “Well, they want
it.” I said, “Gracious,” almost said

00:57:53.960 --> 00:57:59.000
the man's name. I said, “Have you never
read the federalist papers?” That's all

00:57:59.000 --> 00:58:06.000
about how hard these things are and how propitious
the conditions are here.

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:11.960
Churchill was aware that. That's basic political
science. How do you get a regime set up where

00:58:11.960 --> 00:58:17.290
it’ll be stable and just to the weak and
the strong alike? How you’re going to do

00:58:17.290 --> 00:58:23.780
that? Because that's pretty rare. In our own
case it’s influenced by this weird thing

00:58:23.780 --> 00:58:31.030
about us which can never be repeated, and
that is a bunch of people from developed and

00:58:31.030 --> 00:58:39.280
learned countries came over here to a wild
new place and got to start completely over.

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:46.020
They brought huge amounts of stuff with them
and they a built country, a great one eventually.

00:58:46.020 --> 00:58:51.840
It was very hard for them to do it. In places
where the conditions are not like that maybe

00:58:51.840 --> 00:58:56.030
they can't.
Remember Churchill's claim about India, is

00:58:56.030 --> 00:59:02.360
a prudential claim. All of the factors that
I listed mattered to him. He, by the way,

00:59:02.360 --> 00:59:07.980
he was in charge of Iraq for a while. He didn't
want to stay. His number two in command, Lawrence

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:14.530
of Arabia, didn't want to stay either. But
India was like this. We didn't just go conquer

00:59:14.530 --> 00:59:21.900
it. We got it. It's gone very well. Things
are better there now. They accept us. We don't

00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:27.090
rule them with an iron first. We don't need
to. Most of the policing and most of the defense,

00:59:27.090 --> 00:59:33.810
almost all in fact is done by them. We are
introducing government institutions at the

00:59:33.810 --> 00:59:39.950
provincial level that is teaching them the
practices of self-government. You know by

00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:46.550
the way Indians today take pride in those
practices and that they have them. They didn't

00:59:46.550 --> 00:59:53.980
get them from any time before Great Britain.
So colonialism if it was just in Churchill's

00:59:53.980 --> 01:00:02.380
argument would be the seedbed of constitutionalism.
He gave a Fourth of July speech once in his

01:00:02.380 --> 01:00:08.780
life and he said Fourth of July 1918, “By
the declaration of independence we lost an

01:00:08.780 --> 01:00:16.160
empire,” meaning all this, here. “But
by it we've also preserved an Empire, because

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:21.720
we learned that people have the right to govern
themselves and the empire is an instrument

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:29.930
for that.”
Now that might not be. It’s a prudential

01:00:29.930 --> 01:00:36.840
argument, it depends on a bunch of characteristics
being so. That means that Churchill didn't

01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:41.900
think the British Empire should go anywhere.
By the way, if you ever look at a map, first

01:00:41.900 --> 01:00:46.840
of all, if you look at the places where it
was, it was all over, but if you look at the

01:00:46.840 --> 01:00:54.230
map of the places that were important to him
they go around the sea lanes. Churchill was

01:00:54.230 --> 01:01:01.800
interested in that, great maritime power.
He thought British freedom was traceable back

01:01:01.800 --> 01:01:10.010
to the English Channel. That's the point.
The reason it’s interesting today is because

01:01:10.010 --> 01:01:17.480
we've got a problem how we’re going to deal
with parts of the world that are not free

01:01:17.480 --> 01:01:26.560
but are powerful? Churchill's plan was the
British Empire is going to help manage some

01:01:26.560 --> 01:01:33.401
of those places whether in key spots and make
the world better, in conjunction with the

01:01:33.401 --> 01:01:40.020
United States. He wanted a wedding at Fulton
of the strength of America and the British

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:45.810
Empire and Commonwealth. Franklin Roosevelt
and Dwight Eisenhower were reluctant about

01:01:45.810 --> 01:01:54.090
any affiliation with the Empire.
My only point is you can describe his plan

01:01:54.090 --> 01:02:02.160
and you'd have to call it brilliant and deeply
learned and knowledgeable. There might not

01:02:02.160 --> 01:02:08.570
be anything like the right plan today. It
didn't work then in the way he wanted it to.

01:02:08.570 --> 01:02:15.380
But we need some plan like that because the
world is very dangerous and surely it would

01:02:15.380 --> 01:02:30.230
start with being friends with our friends.
One more they say.

01:02:30.230 --> 01:02:36.430
Speaker 7: If Obama is not a Muslim is he
an anti-colonialist?

01:02:36.430 --> 01:02:47.160
Larry: If Obama is not a Muslim is he an anti-colonialist?
I think he said things like that. So explaining

01:02:47.160 --> 01:02:52.811
the mind of Barack Obama, there's a good book
by the way called “I am the change” by

01:02:52.811 --> 01:02:58.010
Charles Kesler, you should read that. What
you should do whenever you’re thinking about

01:02:58.010 --> 01:03:06.510
anybody serious, and Obama is a very serious
man, is try to make a consistency in his thought

01:03:06.510 --> 01:03:13.390
and you'll know that you’ve unlocked it
if you can uncover the good that they love.

01:03:13.390 --> 01:03:19.770
What do they think?
Aristotle says, “Every action aims to the

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:28.940
good, every choice, every thought, everything.”
So you can understand Hitler. Hitler thought

01:03:28.940 --> 01:03:36.600
that he could make a heaven on earth on the
basis of race, at last. It was partly like

01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:46.730
this mystical Norse Aryan history, but it
was also scientific experiments to mess with

01:03:46.730 --> 01:03:54.170
people's genes and to wipe out an entire people.
The communist, what are they thinking? They’re

01:03:54.170 --> 01:04:00.930
thinking that at last we've got to the place
where we can take control of everything and

01:04:00.930 --> 01:04:09.110
we can eliminate everything that's wrong,
we can fix it all. The socialists in Britain

01:04:09.110 --> 01:04:18.140
in the end that's what they're saying. Here's
a claim in the Fabian Essays. The claim is

01:04:18.140 --> 01:04:24.160
if you will just provide everybody with the
same and a sufficient income, everybody will

01:04:24.160 --> 01:04:29.900
be a lot nicer to each other and much more
productive, because they won't have to worry

01:04:29.900 --> 01:04:32.610
about grasping and just getting their own
anymore.

01:04:32.610 --> 01:04:42.670
But where does that idea come from? Their
plan was to implement that flipping idea.

01:04:42.670 --> 01:04:50.070
There had been trials of it by early socialists
and they had all collapsed, but nevermind,

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:56.790
we're going to, it's inevitable. They argue
that democratic politics have come to Britain,

01:04:56.790 --> 01:05:02.270
therefore the people are going to rule and
they're going to elect us, and we are going

01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:09.450
to implement a socialist regime and everybody's
going to love it, and up to step four every

01:05:09.450 --> 01:05:15.260
word of that was an accurate prediction. That's
the point.

01:05:15.260 --> 01:05:21.070
Let's instead of this because here's the way
of thinking if you think the way Churchill

01:05:21.070 --> 01:05:26.210
did, if you think the way Thomas Jefferson
thought, here's what you’d think. You'd

01:05:26.210 --> 01:05:34.040
think we're all here in this room. I know
a lot of you. Some of you are old friends.

01:05:34.040 --> 01:05:39.500
I respect and admire you all as a bunch. I
think of you, when I think of you I think,

01:05:39.500 --> 01:05:44.760
“These are steady people. They've got jobs.
They work. They go through the same struggles

01:05:44.760 --> 01:05:50.740
I do. They've got kids, a lot of them. They
love those kids very much. They're going to

01:05:50.740 --> 01:05:56.330
get old and die and that's sad. And they don't
always do as well as each other, and some

01:05:56.330 --> 01:06:04.080
of that is chance.” This is life. We love
our life because we get a chance to live them.

01:06:04.080 --> 01:06:10.890
You take one's children away and one's job
away, because we've got a guaranteed income,

01:06:10.890 --> 01:06:17.440
and then one's conscience away, because if
every standard of right is evolving over time,

01:06:17.440 --> 01:06:22.480
what do we mean by conscious except some form
of compliance or self-indulgence? Those are

01:06:22.480 --> 01:06:29.050
the two alternatives. If you take those three
things away your conscience and your property

01:06:29.050 --> 01:06:38.200
and your children, what's left of human life?
How do we get to live then? Somebody's going

01:06:38.200 --> 01:06:46.950
to be living for us. Then the next step will
be bread for a purpose, will be bread without

01:06:46.950 --> 01:06:56.700
the ambition to any other purpose.
I think when he was 25 years old I think I

01:06:56.700 --> 01:07:04.680
know the place where it becomes manifest.
Winston Churchill saw that coming. I think

01:07:04.680 --> 01:07:14.490
he fought his entire life to stop it. I think
he didn't win, except a lot he did, and there

01:07:14.490 --> 01:07:24.680
isn't any record of anyone who fought against
that so well and explained so well.

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:32.860
Now you could say these trends are awesome
and they can't be stopped. But he resisted

01:07:32.860 --> 01:07:38.400
that kind of thinking. Although he wrote a
lot about it and entertained the notion that

01:07:38.400 --> 01:07:43.670
that is how the world had come to be, things
are so big now we can't control them. But

01:07:43.670 --> 01:07:50.080
do you know what proves that that's not the
way of things? Because on the 28th of May

01:07:50.080 --> 01:07:56.180
in the year 1940 there was one living man
with the ability to make that speech that

01:07:56.180 --> 01:08:03.270
I just told you about, and that man was in
that place. Everything is different because

01:08:03.270 --> 01:08:13.000
that happened. In other words, he proved the
truth of it himself with his own actions.

01:08:13.000 --> 01:08:14.870
That's why no one must ever despair.

